---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { storyBySlug, storyTitle } from '../../data/stories';
import doorScene1 from '../../assets/security-door-scene1.webp';
import doorScene2 from '../../assets/security-door-scene2.webp';
import doorScene4 from '../../assets/security-door-scene4.webp';
import doorScene6 from '../../assets/security-door-scene6.webp';
import doorScene9 from '../../assets/security-door-scene9.webp';
import doorScene11 from '../../assets/security-door-scene11.webp';
import doorScene14 from '../../assets/security-door-scene14.webp';
import doorScene18 from '../../assets/security-door-scene18.webp';
import doorScene21 from '../../assets/security-door-scene21.webp';
import doorScene26 from '../../assets/security-door-scene26.webp';

// The Security story's permanent Explore route. Thirty-two beats, and
// scene 32 is the end of it.
//
// The opening construction is the one looks-real-is-it established and
// who-chose-what-you-saw reuses: one grid, the copy on the left and the
// picture on the right, centred against each other, stacking copy-first
// below 980px. Same shell, same spacing scale, same serif sizes, same
// turn treatment — nothing global is touched here.
//
// SCENE 1. The card has already said the sentence; the page says it again
// as its own opening, because the second half is the part that has to
// land slowly:
//
//     Security didn't keep changing.
//     What you trusted did.
//     And you probably didn't notice when it happened.
//     Start with your front door →
//
// Nothing is explained and nothing is named. The picture is a front door
// someone is knocking at. The circles around it are cropped and quieted
// on purpose: the visitor should be asking why this ordinary door
// matters, not reading a diagram.
//
// SCENE 2. Everything scene 1 put around the door is taken away, and what
// is left is the door:
//
//     You leave home.
//     You close the door.
//     You lock it.
//     Then you walk away.
//     Why? →
//
// Four things the reader has done a thousand times, in the order they do
// them, and then the question. Nothing is named and nothing is explained,
// because the point of this beat is the answer the reader gives
// themselves before the story gives them one.
//
// The picture is the other supplied asset, whole: an ordinary front door
// on its own. Going back to a plain door after the first scene's
// ecosystem is the beat — the complexity was real, and it is now
// entirely out of sight.
//
// SCENE 3. The same door, closer:
//
//     Because you trust the lock.
//     You don't stay behind to guard the door.
//     You expect the lock to do its job after you've walked away.
//     Then someone else needs to get in →
//
// The one idea the story is willing to name yet is trust, and this is
// where it is named. Still nothing about what a lock is, what it can
// fail at, or who else is involved.
//
// The picture does not change: it is the same element and the same file
// as scene 2, moved in on by a transform rather than swapped for a
// second crop. That is the whole point of the beat — the reader has not
// been shown a new picture, they have stepped closer to the one they
// were already looking at. See .door__frame in the styles.
//
// SCENE 4. Someone else is at the door:
//
//     A pipe starts leaking.
//     You call a plumber.
//     You won't be home when he arrives.
//     So you leave him a key.
//     What changed? →
//
// Four ordinary decisions, none of them careless, and the story does not
// say what they add up to. The reader is the one who notices that
// somebody who is not them can now open the door — which is why the turn
// asks rather than tells.
//
// The picture is the same porch at the same size, with a person at it:
// the file is the Frontdoor asset's exact dimensions, so it lands in the
// window the door already occupied and the beat reads as someone
// arriving at a place the reader knows, not as a new picture. He is
// deliberately unremarkable — work clothes, a toolbag, reaching for the
// handle.
//
// SCENE 5. The first callback to the opening, in the opening's own shape:
//
//     The lock didn't change.
//     Who you trusted did.
//     The same key still opens the same door.
//     But now someone else can use it.
//     Then he arrives →
//
// Deliberately not the opening sentence repeated — the same couplet
// construction with different words, so the rhyme is felt before it is
// recognised. Still no mechanism, still nothing named beyond trust.
//
// The picture stays the plumber and steps forward slightly. The move is
// small on purpose: anything strong enough to actually re-centre on the
// man would have to cut the door or a planter in half, and this beat
// needs the place unchanged, because that is what it says. The sentence
// moves the attention, not the camera.
//
// SCENE 6. Two people are on the step:
//
//     He arrives.
//     But he isn't alone.
//     He brought someone to help.
//     You've never met him.
//     And yet he's about to enter your home. →
//
// Nothing is explained and nothing is named. The second man is in the
// same uniform, carrying the same kind of bag, doing nothing unusual —
// the discomfort has to come from the arithmetic, not from how he looks.
//
// The picture is the third supplied asset, the same porch at the same
// 1122x1402 frame with a second person on it, shown whole and unpushed:
// scene 5 had stepped in slightly, and coming back out is what makes room
// for somebody who was not expected.
//
// NOTE ON THAT FILE. It arrived as a PNG with no alpha — a white matte
// where the other two are cut out. Left as supplied it would render as a
// white rectangle on the page's cream, so the matte was lifted before
// conversion: a flood fill inward from the border over connected
// near-white pixels, with a one-pixel feather. Border-connected only, so
// the lit glass and the lamp are untouched, and no pixel of the subject
// was altered. Composition, colour and framing are exactly as delivered.
//
// SCENE 7. The idea the story has been walking toward, said plainly and
// still not named:
//
//     You didn't choose him.
//     You didn't check him.
//     You don't even know him.
//     But the plumber does.
//     So now you're trusting someone
//     because someone you trust trusts him.
//     And that's only one step away →
//
// The last sentence is the beat, so it gets the story's serif at full
// ink a step down from the headline (.door__lands) and the turn waits
// an extra moment before it appears — the line should be read before
// there is a way onward. No terminology: not vendor, not third party,
// not fourth party, not supply chain. The reader gets the idea first;
// the word for it can come later.
//
// The picture moves onto the two of them — same file, a transform on
// the element already on screen. The door is still behind them but its
// pediment, the lamp and the far planter fall outside the frame, so the
// pair is what the scene is about and the door is what they are standing
// at. Nothing is drawn between them: no arrow, no line, no diagram. The
// sentence is the only thing connecting them.
//
// SCENE 8. The idea stated as plainly as it can be, and still not named:
//
//     Trust can travel.
//     You chose the plumber.
//     He chose someone else.
//     And his choice entered your home too.
//     Now put the key away →
//
// Three sentences and an arithmetic the reader has already done. Still no
// terminology — not vendor, not third or fourth party, not supply chain,
// not dependency, not risk.
//
// The picture eases back out to the window scene 5 used: the whole
// ordinary porch, both men well inside it, nothing singled out and
// nothing drawn between them. Returning to the framing of the beat that
// asked who you trusted is the quiet rhyme — the same view, with two
// people in it now.
//
// SCENE 9. The upgrade, and it is allowed to feel like one:
//
//     You replace the lock.
//     No key this time.
//     Your phone opens the door.
//     It feels simpler.
//     But something else just changed. →
//
// Nothing here says software, network, cloud, vendor or risk. On this
// beat the change genuinely is a convenience — the reader should like it.
// Only the turn hints that there is more to it, and it does not say what.
//
// The picture is the fourth supplied asset: the same door with a keypad
// lock above the handle and a phone held up to it. One change and no
// other: no cloud, no signal, no badge, no diagram, nothing drawn on it.
//
// It is 1181x1332 where the other three door assets are 1122x1402, so it
// sits beside the shared window rather than inside it and keeps its own
// proportions — a frame built for 0.8 would have letterboxed or cropped
// it. Nothing else about the composition changes.
//
// SCENE 10. What the convenience cost, listed and not explained:
//
//     Your door now depends on things you can't see.
//     Your phone.
//     Software.
//     A connection.
//     And none of them stop at your house →
//
// Three nouns, no sentences around them. The story still does not say
// where the software runs, who operates it, or what any of it connects
// to — only that the door no longer works by itself.
//
// The picture moves in on the phone and the lock: x 65-655, y 307-972 of
// the 1181x1332 file, a transform on the element already on screen. The
// two of them and the gap between them are the subject now, but the lit
// glass, the door frame, the wood and the planter are all still in shot,
// so it stays plainly the same door. The dependencies arrive in the
// words; nothing is drawn on the picture — no signal, no cloud, no
// arrow, no label.
//
// SCENE 11. The first thing outside the house:
//
//     Your phone doesn't open the door by itself.
//     Somewhere else, software has to work.
//     A service has to respond.
//     A company has to keep it running.
//     So who are you trusting now? →
//
// Three plain facts and a question. Nothing about cybersecurity, third
// or fourth parties, supply chains, vendor management, or how any of it
// is built. The reader only has to realise that their front door now
// depends on something operated somewhere else.
//
// The picture is the fifth supplied asset: the same door and the same
// phone at the same lock, with one link out to a service and the company
// that runs it. One dependency, not a network of them — no second hop,
// no extra nodes, no labels, nothing drawn on it.
//
// It is landscape (1536x1024) where every other picture here is
// portrait, so this beat gives the copy column exactly the width the
// other beats give it — nothing in the text moves when it lands — and
// hands the picture the rest of the row instead of the narrow portrait
// track, which a 1.5 image would otherwise sit in at half the size.
//
// SCENE 12. Where the trust actually sits now:
//
//     Not just the lock anymore.
//     You're trusting the company behind it.
//     To keep the software working.
//     To keep the service available.
//     To let you back in.
//     And they don't work alone →
//
// One new realisation and no more: the trust moved from an object in the
// house to an organisation outside it. Still nothing about third or
// fourth parties, vendor risk, supply chains or cybersecurity.
//
// The picture shifts onto the company: same file, a transform on the
// element already on screen. The building is the largest thing in frame
// now, but the door, the phone at its lock and the whole run of light
// between them stay in shot — the beat is that the trust moved, not that
// the house did. Nothing is added to the picture: no second company, no
// chain, no label, no icon.
//
// SCENE 13. One more step, and the reader is the one who recognises it:
//
//     The company you trust depends on someone else.
//     Another company helps keep the service running.
//     You may never see them.
//     You may never know their name.
//     But your door depends on them too. →
//
// The same shape as the plumber bringing someone — deliberately not said
// out loud, because the reader made that connection on their own once
// already and will make it again faster. Still no vendor, third party,
// fourth party, supply chain, cybersecurity or risk.
//
// The picture does not move a pixel. That is the beat: the reader is
// told the company depends on somebody else, and the picture shows
// nobody else — the same window, the same door, the same run of light,
// the same one company. The absence is the point, so there is nothing to
// reveal and nothing to move toward.
//
// SCENE 14. The second company, finally shown:
//
//     You never chose them.
//     But the company you trust did.
//     And now part of your front door depends on their choice too.
//     Recognise this pattern? →
//
// The plumber's helper, one layer out, and the turn asks rather than
// tells — the reader has already lived this once and should be the one
// to say so. Still no third party, fourth party, vendor, supply chain,
// cybersecurity or risk.
//
// The picture is the sixth supplied asset: the same door, the same phone
// at the same lock, the same service and the same company, with one more
// company past it. One additional dependency, not a chain — no third
// hop, no labels, no logos, no icons, nothing drawn on it.
//
// It is 1950x807, wider again than anything before it, so like the
// others it keeps its own proportions: the width of the row, its own
// height, no frame and no crop. The copy column stays exactly where the
// other beats put it, so nothing in the text moves.
//
// SCENE 15. The callback, finally made conscious:
//
//     Same pattern. Different scale.
//     You trusted the plumber.
//     He trusted someone else.
//     You trust the company.
//     It trusts someone else.
//     Now give it a name →
//
// Two couplets rather than four lines, the second set a beat behind the
// first and the turn behind both: the parallel is the whole point, so
// the second pair should be felt arriving against the shape of the
// first, and the comparison should be complete before there is anywhere
// to go. Neither relationship is named — that is what the turn offers.
//
// The picture does not change. It has said everything it needs to say
// since scene 14, and this beat is a sentence about the pictures the
// reader has already seen, not a new one.
//
// SCENE 16. The terminology, and only the terminology:
//
//     Now the names make sense.
//     The company you chose?
//     A third party.
//     A company it relies on to serve you?
//     That can be a fourth party.
//     Different names.
//     Same pattern of trust.
//     And it doesn't stop there →
//
// The reader has already lived the structure for fifteen beats, so this
// beat does not define anything — it hands over two words for something
// they already understand. No definitions, no fifth party, no nth party,
// no vendor risk management, no cybersecurity.
//
// Each question takes the aside's quiet type and its answer the story's
// serif at full ink, set tight under it so the two read as one unit (see
// .door__name). These are the only words in the story that are
// terminology, so they are the only ones set like a term. They arrive one
// after the other, then the line that ties them together, then the way
// onward.
//
// The picture does not change, and nothing is written on it: the words
// are where the names belong.
//
// SCENE 17. The scale, felt rather than counted:
//
//     That fourth party can depend on someone else.
//     And they can depend on someone else too.
//     Soon, your front door can rely on companies
//     you never chose,
//     never met,
//     and may never know exist.
//     All for one door to open →
//
// The last sentence is broken across four lines because the break is the
// point: the list keeps going after the reader expects it to stop. No
// fifth party, no nth party, no supply chain, no vendor ecosystem, no
// cybersecurity — the reader should feel how far this goes before anyone
// counts it.
//
// The picture leans toward the far end: the second company gains a little
// size and sits nearer the edge of the frame, while the door, its lock,
// the link and the first company all stay in shot. The relationship has
// to stay complete for a sentence about it continuing to mean anything,
// and no company is added — what continues, continues past the frame.
//
// SCENE 18. The scale, drawn once:
//
//     One door. Many others behind it.
//     You chose one company.
//     That company chose another.
//     And that one chose another.
//     But your door still depends on all of them.
//     There's a name for this →
//
// The three choices are one run, so they arrive as one block; the line
// that turns it back on the reader stands apart, and the turn comes
// last. Plain words throughout — nothing here needs a second reading.
// Still nothing named: no supply chain, no ecosystem, no nth party, no vendor
// risk, no cybersecurity. The reader sees how far it reaches before
// anyone tells them what it is called.
//
// The picture is the seventh supplied asset: the door and its lock, the
// service, and the companies running back from it until they are too far
// off to make out. Whole and unpushed — its own 1672x941, the width of
// the row, its own height, no frame, no crop, nothing added. The door is
// exactly as simple as it was in scene 2; only what stands behind it has
// changed, which is the whole point of the frame.
//
// SCENE 19. The name, for something the reader has already watched for
// eighteen beats:
//
//     This is a supply chain.
//     One company relies on another.
//     That one relies on another.
//     And your door depends on the whole chain.
//     Now look at what changed →
//
// The term is the headline, because the headline is where this story puts
// the thing that matters — no definition under it, no second sentence
// explaining what a supply chain is. Two lines restating the shape in the
// plainest words available, then the consequence. Nothing about
// supply-chain risk, vendor risk, cyber supply chain, fifth or nth
// parties, or cybersecurity: the reader should think "oh, that's what
// that means", not "now I am being taught something".
//
// The picture does not change. It already drew the structure a beat ago,
// and this beat only tells the reader what the thing they are looking at
// is called.
//
// SCENE 20. The whole story in four steps:
//
//     The door didn't change.
//     What it depends on did.
//     A key became a phone.
//     A phone needed software.
//     The software needed companies.
//     And those companies needed others.
//     Same door. More things can go wrong →
//
// The opening line is the story's own shape again — the thing itself did
// not change, what stands behind it did — and then four steps, each one
// handing off to the next, so the growth is felt as a sequence rather
// than summarised. Plain words, and still nothing about cyber risk,
// vendor risk, supply-chain risk or AI.
//
// The picture does not change. It has shown the progression since scene
// 18; this beat says what the reader has been looking at the whole time.
//
// SCENE 21. What the chain means when one link stops:
//
//     A problem there can become a problem here.
//     One company stops working.
//     Another depends on it.
//     And something at your home can stop working too.
//     Now the word "risk" makes sense →
//
// Cause, link, consequence — three plain statements, and the word the
// reader is about to be given is still only in the turn. Nothing about
// supply-chain risk, cyber risk, vendor risk or third-party risk: they
// should understand what risk means here before anybody names it.
//
// The picture is the same chain as the beat before, with the far end
// gone dark and the line of light fading out before it gets there.
// Nothing is drawn on it to say so — no warning mark, no red, no
// exclamation, no label. The lights are simply off at that end, and the
// door is as warm and ordinary as it has been since scene 2.
//
// Whole and unpushed at its own 2172x724 — the fifth shape in this
// story, taking the width of the row and its own height, no frame, no
// crop.
//
// SCENE 22. The second naming, on the same terms as the first:
//
//     This is supply chain risk.
//     A problem starts somewhere else.
//     It moves through the chain.
//     And eventually, it can reach you.
//     And that's not just about doors →
//
// The term is the headline and there is no definition under it — three
// plain sentences restating what the reader has already watched happen,
// the same construction scene 19 used to name the chain itself. Nothing
// about cybersecurity, AI security, vendor risk management or
// third-party risk management.
//
// The picture does not change. It showed the problem travelling a beat
// ago; this beat only says what that is called.
//
// SCENE 23. The door lets go of the story:
//
//     The door was only the easy example.
//     The same thing happens with
//     your data,
//     your identity,
//     your work,
//     and the systems you rely on every day.
//     The things change. The pattern doesn't →
//
// The list is the beat — it is the door replaced four times over, so each
// thing gets its own line and the reader adds them up. Nothing is
// explained about any of them, and nothing is named: no cybersecurity,
// no AI security, no physical security, no risk management of any kind.
//
// The picture does not change, and deliberately so: the words widen the
// idea while the reader is still looking at the one small example it
// came from.
//
// SCENE 24. The story read back, in the order the reader lived it:
//
//     What you trust changes with them.
//     First, a lock.
//     Then a person.
//     Then a phone.
//     Then a company.
//     Then companies behind that company.
//     And we gave each new problem a name →
//
// One accumulating run, so it arrives as one block rather than five
// statements: every line the reader has already watched happen, in
// sequence, with nothing added to any of them. Still nothing named — no
// physical security, no cybersecurity, no third-party risk, no
// supply-chain risk, no AI security, no vendor risk. The names are what
// the turn offers.
//
// The picture does not change. The chain is what all five lines are
// about, and it has been on screen since scene 21.
//
// SCENE 25. The names, four at once:
//
//     Different problems. Different names.
//     Protecting the door?                       Physical security.
//     Connecting it to software?                 Cybersecurity.
//     Depending on another company?              Third-party risk.
//     Depending on the companies behind them?    Supply chain risk.
//     Then we let something make decisions for us →
//
// Not a glossary. The reader has already lived every one of these, so
// each gets a question and a word and nothing else — the same
// construction scene 16 used for the first two names, with a tighter
// stagger because there are four of them. Nothing about AI security, AI
// risk, governance or vendor risk management: AI comes next, and it is
// the one change the reader has not been walked through yet.
//
// The picture does not change, and nothing is written on it: the words
// are where the names belong, exactly as in scene 16.
//
// SCENE 26. The door again, and something at it that decides:
//
//     Now the system can make a judgement.
//     A camera sees a face.
//     Software compares it.
//     Familiar or not?
//     The answer can change what happens next.
//     That's a different kind of trust →
//
// One realization and no more: every beat until now was about trusting
// something to work. This is the first about trusting something to
// judge. So no AI, no model, no algorithm, no machine learning, no
// governance, and no AI risk — the shift is meant to be felt a beat
// before it has a name.
//
// The picture leaves the chain and returns to the door the story opened
// on, with the same one person at it. What is new is in the artwork and
// nowhere else: nothing is drawn on top of it, nothing is labelled, and
// nothing is flagged.
//
// SCENE 27. The difference stated plainly:
//
//     Before, you trusted things to work.
//     Now you're trusting something to decide.
//     Who gets in.
//     Who doesn't.
//     What happens next.
//     Now give that a name →
//
// One realization: trusting something to work is not the same as
// trusting something to decide. The three lines under it are the
// shortest in the story on purpose — the beat is the difference itself,
// and nothing should sit between the reader and it. Still no AI, no
// model, no algorithm, no machine learning, no governance: the turn is
// what offers the name.
//
// The same picture as scene 26, moved in on the thing the sentence is
// about. No second asset, nothing drawn on it, nothing labelled.
//
// SCENE 28. The name, after twenty-seven beats of the thing itself:
//
//     This is where AI enters the story.
//     Until now, you trusted things to work.
//     Now you're also trusting what they decide.
//     The camera sees.
//     The system compares.
//     A judgement changes what happens next.
//     So what are you trusting now? →
//
// The first two letters of AI in the whole story, and they arrive as a
// headline over something the reader has already watched happen twice.
// One realization and no more: AI changes the kind of trust involved.
// So nothing yet about AI security, AI risk, bias, hallucination, model
// security or governance, and no machine-learning vocabulary anywhere —
// this beat names the shift, it does not start cataloguing it.
//
// "Now you're also trusting what they decide" takes the full-ink serif
// .door__lands gives a conclusion, because that is where the thought
// turns; the rest stays in the aside's quiet type.
//
// The picture holds scene 27's framing without moving a pixel. The words
// carry the reveal, and nothing is drawn on the artwork to help them: no
// label, no network graphic, no diagram, no overlay, no warning mark.
//
// SCENE 29. What there now is to protect, and what does not count:
//
//     Now you trust the decision too.
//     Getting it wrong is one problem.
//     Being fooled on purpose is another.
//     If someone can manipulate the decision,
//     that's a security question.
//     That's a new security question →
//
// One realization: once a system starts making decisions, protecting the
// decision becomes part of security too — but only the second half of
// that is a security question. A system making an ordinary mistake is
// not automatically a security problem; the security question is the one
// where somebody can influence the decision on purpose. An earlier draft
// of this beat asked three questions that were all about the answer
// being wrong and then called that "a new security question", which read
// as though every mistake were one.
//
// The distinction is drawn in the plainest words available: wrong,
// fooled, manipulate. No adversarial AI, no spoofing, no model attacks,
// no prompt injection, no bias, no accuracy metrics, nothing technical.
//
// The name is still one beat away: nothing here about AI security, AI
// risk, adversarial AI, model security, prompt injection, bias or
// governance. The reader should have the problem before they have the
// word for it.
//
// The picture holds the same framing, and stays calm and ordinary. No
// attack graphic, no intruder, no warning mark, no red — the questions
// are asked about an unremarkable afternoon, which is the point.
//
// SCENE 30. The judgement tied back to the word the story is named for:
//
//     AI changes the security question.
//     Before, you protected what the system could access.
//     Now you also have to protect how it decides.
//     Now look back at where we started →
//
// This beat does one thing: it connects the judgement the reader has
// just watched to security. It does not open anything new — no bias, no
// hallucination, no governance, no model attacks, no prompt injection,
// no risk framework. The story is closing, and a beat that started a
// fresh subject here would stop it closing.
//
// "Now you also have to protect how it decides" takes the full-ink serif
// .door__lands keeps for a conclusion, the same construction scene 28
// used — this is the same sentence finishing its thought. Nothing
// follows it, so the turn comes straight after rather than waiting on an
// echo the way scene 28's did.
//
// The picture holds the same framing. Nothing new is drawn on it.
//
// SCENE 31. The callback:
//
//     Different names. Same pattern.
//     Physical security.
//     Cybersecurity.
//     Third-party risk.
//     Supply chain risk.
//     AI security.
//     Different places for things to go wrong.
//     Different things to trust.
//     Now read the first line again →
//
// The five names on their own, with no question above any of them.
// Scene 25 already taught what they refer to; teaching the mappings a
// second time here made the ending a lesson rather than a gathering-up,
// and made the two lists read as the story repeating itself. What the
// beat adds instead is what the names have in common — different places
// for things to go wrong, different things to trust — in the aside's
// quiet type, so it reads as the reflection on the list rather than a
// sixth name. No definitions and no explanations.
//
// Each name keeps its own paragraph, so each still arrives on its own
// beat at the timings scene 31 already used.
//
// The picture is scene 1's composition, the same element and the same
// file, whole and unscaled. The reader met all of it in the first beat
// before knowing what any of it was for; it comes back unlabelled and
// unannotated, because by now it does not need explaining.
//
// SCENE 32. The end:
//
//     Security didn't keep changing.
//     What you trusted did.
//     And every new thing you depend on
//     gives security something new to protect.
//     See it differently.
//
// The first two lines are the opening, unchanged, in the opening's own
// construction — the same element, the same size, the second half in the
// same softer ink. Nothing marks them as a callback and nothing explains
// what is different about them, because what is different is the reader.
// The list they walked to get here is not read back.
//
// No picture. Every image in this file belongs to a beat, this beat
// names none of them, so the whole visual column goes — the last screen
// is the one the reader should sit with, and the room is most of what
// says so. Scene 31 already did the visual callback; doing it twice
// would make the ending another step instead of a stop.
//
// The block is a single 600px column, centred in the row and centred in
// what is left of the screen, with the text still ranged left inside it.
// Ranged left because this is the same voice the story has used for
// thirty-one beats; centred as a block because empty space only reads as
// deliberate when it falls on both sides. Left where the copy column had
// been, the ending read as a page with half of it missing.
//
// The rhythm is statement / moderate gap / supporting thought / larger
// gap / signature: the supporting line belongs to the statement above it
// rather than standing as its own section, and the signature needs air
// without being marooned.
//
// "See it differently." is a signature, not a control: the story's serif
// at full ink, no saffron circle, no arrow, nothing to press. The only
// thing after it is a real link back to Explore — navigation after the
// story has ended rather than the last thing the story says, so it is
// smaller than the aside type, quieter than it, and further down than
// anything else in the beat. The way on is another story, the same
// convention did-your-face-leave-the-phone.astro ends on.
//
// The beat has no turn, so focus lands on .door__lines, which is what
// that element and its tabindex are for.
//
// This is the last beat. There is no scene 33, and the story does not
// continue past it.
//
// Still deliberately absent until the story is finished: a
// LearningResource, a share card, and a sitemap entry (see NOT_INDEXABLE
// in astro.config.mjs). The manifest's question, shortAnswer and
// keyTakeaway are still marked provisional there, and are still rendered
// nowhere.
const story = storyBySlug('security-didnt-keep-changing')!;
